  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Coastal states stability mechanism (CSSM)Coastal states stability mechanism (CSSM)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Violent extremist organizations (VEO) operating from Sahel are increasingly expanding across borders into northern regions of Coastal West African (CWA) countries. Their expansion is facilitated by destabilizing factors in periphery border communities, such as long-standing patterns and perceptions of exclusion, weak state presence and a lack of economic prospects. Further, VEO expansion feeds into a self-reinforcing dynamic when the insecurity VEOs generate pushes a gradual state retreat and limits access by humanitarian and development actors. CWA countries have taken steps to respond to the threat, including political, security and development-oriented action. The Coastal States Stability Mechanism (CSSM) contributes to government strategies by creating conditions for stronger and locally accepted state presence that is responsive to community priorities. To achieve its objective, the CSSM is working in a limited number of geographic zones selected based on government security and stabilization priorities. In each location, CSSM is working with government and community partners under three stabilization outcomes relating to (1) critical civilian security services, (2) essential social services, and (3) social and economic engagements.</narrative>
  </description>
